    "body": "Maybe re-word this because when there is a mode change, it's not actually a stack pointer swap. Suggestion:\r\n\r\nWhen changing from S-mode to M-mode, the stack pointer is stored into ssp and loaded from msp, without affecting ssp.PUSH and ssp.PPUSH. \r\nThe reverse happens when changing from M-mode to S-mode. \r\n\r\nI'm not convinced that what I wrote about preserving PUSH and PPUSH is correct; maybe I am getting greedy but perhaps 1 or 2 spare bits of xsp can be used to indicate the PUSH and PPUSH values that should be loaded on a mode-change.   \r\n",
