    "body": "There are a couple of problems with automatic stack pointer swapping on mode change (if that swapping occurs on trap and return, and is unconnected to the mcspspush and mcspspop instructions. \r\nThat is, mcspspush and mcspspop specify a rs and rd, which means any GPR could be the target of the swap. SP swapping that's not connected to these instructions can only target a fixed SP GPR (i.e. the ABI-defined one). \r\nThis is not a major problem, because ABI register definitions are very stable - but probably needs a note. \r\n\r\nAlso, if there is implicit swapping on mode-change, mret may cause a swap when mstatus.MPP does not indicate M-mode. \r\n\r\nAlternatively, the SP manipulation could still be explicit in mcspspush and mcspspop, with extended behaviour e.g. for mcspspop: \r\nif (mstatus.mpp == PRIV_S)  /* expect return to supervisor mode \\*/ \r\n  msp.SP = rs  /* save the M-mode stack pointer with lower bits cleared \\*/\r\n  rd = ssp.SP   /* restore the S-mode stack pointer with lower bits cleared \\*/\r\nelse /* expect return staying in machine mode \\*/\r\n  msp.PUSH = msp.PPUSH \r\n  ...\r\n  ...",
